INSERT INTO rag_documents (document_id,repository_index,repository_name,repository_slug,repository_url,retrieval_class,semantic_area,evidence_polarity,evidence_level,specificity_score,concrete_signal_count,word_count,text,topics_json,evidence_areas_json,related_skill_ratings_json,source_fragments_json,provenance_json) VALUES ('repo-015-rd004',15,'Go-Back-N-Protocol',NULL,NULL,'interpretation','other_repository_evidence','positive','repository_specific',0.3552,7,300,'Section: Go-Back-N Window Logic > Sending

piggybacked acknowledgement.

It then invokes the physical-layer hook and starts a timer.

Section: Event-Driven Protocol Loop

protocol5() switches on protocol events.

Section: Event-Driven Protocol Loop > Network layer ready

A packet is accepted into the sender buffer, sent, and the sender''s upper window edge advances.

Section: Event-Driven Protocol Loop > Frame arrival

An in-order frame is delivered upward and the expected receive sequence advances.

Piggybacked acknowledgement state then contracts the sender window.

Section: Event-Driven Protocol Loop > Checksum error event

The event is represented explicitly in the protocol event system.

Section: Event-Driven Protocol Loop > Timeout

The sender resets next_frame_to_send to the oldest unacknowledged sequence and retransmits all currently buffered outstanding frames.

That is the defining Go-Back-N retransmission behavior.

Section: Flow Control

The network layer is enabled when sender buffering has room and disabled when the sender window is full.

This represents backpressure between the protocol and the layer above it.

Section: Console Simulation

GoBackN.cpp provides a more directly observable simulation.

Section: Console Simulation > User-provided frames

The user enters:

number of frames;
a value for each frame.

Section: Console Simulation > Simulated loss/error

For each transmission, a random number is used to produce an approximately 10% error event.

Section: Console Simulation > Acknowledgement progression

Acknowledgements are printed according to window progress.

Section: Console Simulation > Window retransmission

When an error occurs:

the error location is stored;

frames continue through the current window;

successful earlier frames receive acknowledgement output;

the frame index is reset to the failed frame;

the program prints Retransmitting Window;

transmission resumes from that point.

Section: Console Simulation > Delayed output

usleep is wrapped as delay_ms, making the console simulation unfold visibly over time.

Section: Technical Stack > C++

C++ is used for:

protocol structs;

enums;

