INSERT INTO rag_documents (document_id,repository_index,repository_name,repository_slug,repository_url,retrieval_class,semantic_area,evidence_polarity,evidence_level,specificity_score,concrete_signal_count,word_count,text,topics_json,evidence_areas_json,related_skill_ratings_json,source_fragments_json,provenance_json) VALUES ('repo-026-rd003',26,'Sensors',NULL,NULL,'interpretation','other_repository_evidence','neutral','repository_specific',0.3447,12,188,'Section: What This Project Is

Sensors is a study repository for autonomous-vehicle sensing.

The radar notebook explores how automotive radar range and velocity are derived from physical and signal-processing relationships.

The LIDAR project processes point clouds into road/obstacle representations and object clusters using PCL.

Together these strands expose two complementary perception modalities:

radar for range, beat-frequency, Doppler, and frequency-domain reasoning;
LIDAR for dense 3D point-cloud filtering, segmentation, clustering, and bounding-box generation.

The repository is not simply conceptual notes: it contains executable calculations, executed notebook outputs, C++ processing functions, streamed PCD playback, and documented simulation images.

Section: Project Scope > Radar Study

Radar_.ipynb covers:

carrier frequency;

wavelength;

transmitted power;

antenna gain;

minimum detectable power;

radar cross section;

maximum radar range;

range resolution;

chirp bandwidth;

chirp sweep time;

beat frequency;

target range estimation;

Doppler frequency shift;

radial velocity estimation;

synthetic time-domain signals;

injected noise;

FFT-based frequency analysis.

Section: Project Scope > LIDAR Study

The C++ project covers:

simulated LIDAR scanning;

point-cloud visualization;

voxel-grid downsampling;

region-of-interest cropping;

ego-vehicle roof removal;

plane/obstacle separation;

RANSAC plane segmentation through PCL;

KD-tree-backed Euclidean clustering;

3D bounding boxes;

PCD save/load;

chronological PCD streaming;

repeated frame playback;','["radar","lidar","beat-frequency","kd-tree","scope","study"]','["What This Project Is","Project Scope"]','[]','[{"block_id":"repo-026-s004-b001","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":1,"evidence_level":"methodology_or_interpretive","section_id":"s004","section_path":["What This Project Is"],"section_title":"What This Project Is","source_file":"repositories/repo-026-Sensors/README.md","source_line_end":58,"source_line_start":58,"specificity_score":0.28,"template_repository_frequency":1,"text":"`Sensors` is a study repository for autonomous-vehicle sensing.","text_sha256":"77e3269f99c5e0b4db2c846722db297ce5f7a4f9c7a529e32f59440c5e8856be","unit_id":"s004","word_count":8},{"block_id":"repo-026-s004-b002","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":1,"evidence_level":"methodology_or_interpretive","section_id":"s004","section_path":["What This Project Is"],"section_title":"What This Project Is","source_file":"repositories/repo-026-Sensors/README.md","source_line_end":60,"source_line_start":60,"specificity_score":0.28,"template_repository_frequency":1,"text":"The radar notebook explores how automotive radar range and velocity are derived from physical and signal-processing relationships.","text_sha256":"30de402e782c6f7694120874d7951dbc21f917b414ecbe18dac6906625d8a597","unit_id":"s004","word_count":17},{"block_id":"repo-026-s004-b003","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":1,"evidence_level":"methodology_or_interpretive","section_id":"s004","section_path":["What This Project Is"],"section_title":"What This Project Is","source_file":"repositories/repo-026-Sensors/README.md","source_line_end":62,"source_line_start":62,"specificity_score":0.28,"template_repository_frequency":1,"text":"The LIDAR project processes point clouds into road/obstacle representations and object clusters using PCL.","text_sha256":"74f05cfa2d16d8341361ba690d390b74df2fa0880cc26d7507def1e922a43740","unit_id":"s004","word_count":14},{"block_id":"repo-026-s004-b004","canonica