INSERT INTO rag_documents (document_id,repository_index,repository_name,repository_slug,repository_url,retrieval_class,semantic_area,evidence_polarity,evidence_level,specificity_score,concrete_signal_count,word_count,text,topics_json,evidence_areas_json,related_skill_ratings_json,source_fragments_json,provenance_json) VALUES ('repo-026-rd007',26,'Sensors',NULL,NULL,'interpretation','other_repository_evidence','positive','repository_specific',0.3884,18,325,'Section: Radar Engineering Work > Beat-Frequency Range Estimation

13 MHz → 143 m;

24 MHz → 264 m.

This is direct hands-on evidence of FMCW-style beat-frequency/range reasoning.

Section: Radar Engineering Work > Doppler Velocity Estimation

The notebook uses wavelength and Doppler frequency shift to calculate radial velocity.

Executed examples include both positive and negative shifts, preserving direction-sensitive velocity results.

Section: Radar Engineering Work > Synthetic Signal and Noise

A sampled signal is built from two sinusoids at distinct frequencies and then corrupted with random noise.

This creates a concrete input for frequency-domain inspection rather than discussing FFT only conceptually.

Section: Radar Engineering Work > FFT Analysis

The notebook explicitly transitions to FFT-based frequency analysis of the sampled signal.

Section: LIDAR Engineering Work > Simulated Highway Scanning

simpleHighway initializes an ego vehicle and other cars.

The code creates a Lidar object, performs a scan, obtains a PCL point cloud, segments the road plane, clusters obstacle points, and renders clusters/bounding boxes.

Task comments in the file document the exercised sequence.

Section: LIDAR Engineering Work > Voxel-Grid Filtering

FilterCloud applies pcl::VoxelGrid with a configurable leaf size.

This reduces point density before later processing stages.

Section: LIDAR Engineering Work > Region-of-Interest Cropping

A pcl::CropBox keeps points inside configured spatial boundaries.

This focuses processing on the road region relevant to the vehicle.

Section: LIDAR Engineering Work > Ego-Vehicle Roof Removal

A second crop box identifies the roof region.

Those indices are passed to pcl::ExtractIndices with negative extraction so points belonging to the sensing vehicle are removed from the working cloud.

Section: LIDAR Engineering Work > Plane Segmentation

SegmentPlane configures pcl::SACSegmentation for:

coefficient optimization;

SACMODEL_PLANE;

SAC_RANSAC;

configured maximum iterations;

configured distance threshold.

The inlier set is then separated from obstacle points.

Section: LIDAR Engineering Work > Road / Obstacle Separation

SeparateClouds constructs:

a point cloud containing plane inliers;
an obstacle cloud containing the remaining points.

Section: LIDAR Engineering Work > Euclidean Clustering

Clustering builds a PCL KD tree and configures pcl::EuclideanClusterExtraction.','["radar","lidar","beat-frequency","engineering","work","range","estimation","doppler","velocity","synthetic","signal","noise","simulated","highway","scanning","voxel-grid","filtering","region-of-interest","cropping","ego-vehicle","roof","removal","plane","segmentation"]','["Radar Engineering Work","LIDAR Engineering Work"]','[]','[{"block_id":"repo-026-s025-b005","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":1,"evidence_level":"interpretive","section_id":"s025","section_path":["Radar Engineering Work","Beat-Frequency Range Estimation"],"section_title":"Beat-Frequency Range Estimation","source_file":"repositories/repo-026-Sensors/README.md","source_line_end":270,"source_line_start":270,"specificity_score":0.38,"template_repository_frequency":0,"text":"- 13 MHz → 143 m;","text_sha256":"ffb46215c5c1972daef279ba37d59975b59fcf36de16792435c006dda6473b93","unit_id":"s025","word_count":6},{"block_id":"repo-026-s025-b006","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":1,"evidence_level":"interpretive","section_id":"s025","section_path":["Radar Engineering Work","Beat-Frequency Range Estimation"],"section_title":"Beat-Frequency Range Estimation","source_file":"repositories/repo-026-Sensors/README.md","source_line_end":271,"source_line_start":271,"specificity_score":0.38,"template_repository_frequency":0,"text":"- 24 MHz → 264 m.","text_sha256":"c56176103c60f802cea57e96f9fd09c5a3e18a8ec8680c5ae47f004b331eaee5","unit_id":"s025","word_count":6},{"block_id":"repo-026-s025-b007","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":1,"evidence_level":"interpretive","section_id":"s025","section_path":["Radar Engineering Work","Beat-Frequency Range Estimation"],"section_title":"Beat-Frequency Range Estimation","source_file":"repositories/repo-026-Sensors/README.md","source_line_end":273,"source_line_start":273,"specificity_score":0.38,"template_repository_frequency":1,"text":"This is direct hands-on evidence of FMCW-style 