INSERT INTO rag_documents (document_id,repository_index,repository_name,repository_slug,repository_url,retrieval_class,semantic_area,evidence_polarity,evidence_level,specificity_score,concrete_signal_count,word_count,text,topics_json,evidence_areas_json,related_skill_ratings_json,source_fragments_json,provenance_json) VALUES ('repo-034-rd002',34,'STOP-WATCH-AVR',NULL,NULL,'interpretation','other_repository_evidence','positive','repository_specific',0.3864,15,332,'Section: What This Project Is

STOP-WATCH-AVR implements a digital stopwatch directly against AVR peripheral registers.

The application maintains hours, minutes, and seconds, advances the clock from a Timer1 compare-match interrupt, multiplexes six seven-segment digits, and uses three external interrupts for reset, pause, and resume behavior.

The repository also contains a Proteus project, so the project is not only source code: it preserves a hardware-simulation representation of the microcontroller circuit.

Section: Functional Scope

The implemented stopwatch supports seconds counting, minute rollover, hour rollover, six displayed decimal digits, reset to 00:00:00, pause, resume, and continuously multiplexed seven-segment output.

The source keeps three explicit state variables: seconds, minutes, and hours. That makes the temporal model small and directly inspectable.

Section: Foreground Display Loop

main() repeatedly calls s7seg().

s7seg() activates one display position at a time through PORTA, writes the decimal digit through PORTC, waits approximately 3 ms, and advances to the next position.

The six positions correspond to seconds units, seconds tens, minutes units, minutes tens, hours units, and hours tens.

This is explicit display multiplexing rather than dedicating a separate complete output bus to every digit.

Section: Timer1 Timebase

Timer1_CTC_Init() configures Timer1 in compare mode.

The implementation resets TCNT1, loads OCR1A = 15625, enables the output-compare-A interrupt, enables CTC behavior with WGM12, and selects a timer prescaler through CS11 and CS10.

The compare ISR becomes the application''s timekeeping heartbeat.

Section: Time Rollover Logic

TIMER1_COMPA_vect updates time hierarchically.

When seconds reach 59, seconds return to zero and minutes increment.

When minutes also reach 59, minutes return to zero and hours increment.

The rollover rules are encoded explicitly rather than delegated to a time library.

Section: External Interrupt Controls > INT0 — Reset

INT0_vect sets seconds, minutes, and hours to zero.

Section: External Interrupt Controls > INT1 — Pause

INT1_vect disables the Timer1 compare interrupt by clearing the corresponding TIMSK bit.

The timer configuration can stay intact while stopwatch state stops advancing.

Section: External Interrupt Controls > INT2 — Resume

INT2_vect re-enables the Timer1 compare interrupt.','["proteus","functional","scope","foreground","display","loop","timer1","timebase","time","rollover","logic","external","interrupt","controls","int0","reset","int1","pause","int2","resume"]','["What This Project Is","Functional Scope","Foreground Display Loop","Timer1 Timebase","Time Rollover Logic","External Interrupt Controls"]','[]','[{"block_id":"repo-034-s002-b001","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":2,"evidence_level":"repository_specific","section_id":"s002","section_path":["What This Project Is"],"section_title":"What This Project Is","source_file":"repositories/repo-034-STOP-WATCH-AVR/README.md","source_line_end":16,"source_line_start":16,"specificity_score":0.58,"template_repository_frequency":1,"text":"`STOP-WATCH-AVR` implements a digital stopwatch directly against AVR peripheral registers.","text_sha256":"b030dc8bb1cd459c7fbbcd3aa82c68b48ecda064f6c9e8112981ecaad22cec1e","unit_id":"s002","word_count":10},{"block_id":"repo-034-s002-b002","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":0,"evidence_level":"methodology_or_interpretive","section_id":"s002","section_path":["What This Project Is"],"section_title":"What This Project Is","source_file":"repositories/repo-034-STOP-WATCH-AVR/README.md","source_line_end":18,"source_line_start":18,"specificity_score":0.24,"template_repository_frequency":1,"text":"The application maintains hours, minutes, and seconds, advances the clock from a Timer1 compare-match interrupt, multiplexes six seven-segment digits, and uses three external interrupts for reset, pause, and resume behavior.","text_sha256":"68f70c04662c7c2a7cf471618fafbe1ce149dedefaa5f1c90b800b8dc0cb3261","unit_id":"s002","word_count":30},{"block_id":"repo-034-s002-b003","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":1,"evidence_level":"methodology_or_interpretive","section_id":"s002","section_path":["What This Project Is"],"section_title":"What This Project Is","source_file":"repositories/repo-034-STOP-WATCH-AVR