INSERT INTO rag_documents (document_id,repository_index,repository_name,repository_slug,repository_url,retrieval_class,semantic_area,evidence_polarity,evidence_level,specificity_score,concrete_signal_count,word_count,text,topics_json,evidence_areas_json,related_skill_ratings_json,source_fragments_json,provenance_json) VALUES ('repo-034-rd004',34,'STOP-WATCH-AVR',NULL,NULL,'interpretation','other_repository_evidence','positive','repository_specific',0.3563,10,376,'Section: External Interrupt Controls > INT2 — Resume

Pause and resume therefore manipulate delivery of the timing event rather than reconstructing the timer configuration.

Section: Seven-Segment Multiplexing

PORTA serves as the digit-selection side and PORTC carries the decimal value for the selected digit.

For each position the code selects one display, calculates the relevant decimal digit, writes it, delays briefly, and moves to the next display.

Decimal extraction uses modulo and division directly on the stopwatch counters, for example seconds % 10 for units and tens reconstruction from the remaining value.

Section: Simulation Evidence

The repository contains proteus/mytest.pdsprj.

That is concrete evidence that the stopwatch was paired with a Proteus circuit/simulation project rather than existing only as isolated C source.

The source is naturally observable through timer-driven count progression, interrupt-driven control, and six visible seven-segment outputs.

Section: Engineering Skills Demonstrated > Interrupt-Driven Design

Timekeeping and user controls are handled through interrupt service routines rather than foreground polling alone.

Section: Engineering Skills Demonstrated > Timer Configuration

Timer1 compare-match behavior is used as a deterministic software timebase.

Section: Engineering Skills Demonstrated > Digital Display Driving

Six display digits are multiplexed through shared output resources.

Section: Engineering Skills Demonstrated > Hardware/Software Co-Design

Firmware is paired with a Proteus simulation artifact.

Section: Engineering Skills Demonstrated > State Management

Time state is compact and deterministic, with explicit rollover rules.

Section: Capability Developed

The core capability is coordinating asynchronous interrupt events, persistent state, timer peripherals, display refresh, and hardware register configuration.

Section: Portfolio Evolution

Chronologically, this repository appears immediately after Port-AUTOSAR-ARM.

It shifts from configurable driver-layer work to a compact end application where timers, interrupts, GPIO, and display behavior are assembled into one visible device.

It is the earliest processed repository where an AVR application combines interrupt-driven timekeeping with explicit six-digit seven-segment multiplexing.

Section: Overall Project Narrative

STOP-WATCH-AVR is a small but complete hardware-facing application.

A Timer1 compare interrupt advances the stopwatch independently of the foreground display loop. Three external interrupts provide reset, pause, and resume controls. The foreground continuously multiplexes six seven-segment positions through shared ports. A Proteus project preserves the circuit-level simulation context.

The repository captures a classic embedded pattern: hardware timers create deterministic events, ISRs modify shared state, and a foreground loop renders that state to a physical interface.','["gpio","proteus","external","interrupt","controls","int2","resume","seven-segment","multiplexing","simulation","engineering","skills","demonstrated","interrupt-driven","design","timer","configuration","digital","display","driving","hardware/software","co-design","state","management"]','["External Interrupt Controls","Seven-Segment Multiplexing","Simulation Evidence","Engineering Skills Demonstrated","Capability Developed","Portfolio Evolution","Overall Project Narrative"]','[]','[{"block_id":"repo-034-s013-b002","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":0,"evidence_level":"interpretive","section_id":"s013","section_path":["External Interrupt Controls","INT2 — Resume"],"section_title":"INT2 — Resume","source_file":"repositories/repo-034-STOP-WATCH-AVR/README.md","source_line_end":86,"source_line_start":86,"specificity_score":0.34,"template_repository_frequency":1,"text":"Pause and resume therefore manipulate delivery of the timing event rather than reconstructing the timer configuration.","text_sha256":"adb08ff99bc9359523a33ff2d7e4aab4ae40fd311d669e2db9a79d2733184801","unit_id":"s013","word_count":16},{"block_id":"repo-034-s014-b001","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":1,"evidence_level":"interpretive","section_id":"s014","section_path":["Seven-Segment Multiplexing"],"section_title":"Seven-Segment Multiplexing","source_file":"repositories/repo-034-STOP-WATCH-AVR/README.md","source_line_end":90,"source_line_start":90,"specificity_score":0.38,"template_repository_frequency":1,"text":"`PORTA` serves as the digit-selection side and `PORTC` carries the decimal value for the selected digit.","text_sha256":"17ba92cf30799e9d3f8013d8a97610411af29d79783ea097288ecbef7d5a93b9","unit_id":"s014","word_count":16},{"block_id":"repo-034-s014-b002","canonical_categories":[],"concrete_hits":