INSERT INTO rag_documents (document_id,repository_index,repository_name,repository_slug,repository_url,retrieval_class,semantic_area,evidence_polarity,evidence_level,specificity_score,concrete_signal_count,word_count,text,topics_json,evidence_areas_json,related_skill_ratings_json,source_fragments_json,provenance_json) VALUES ('repo-087-rd004',87,'NLP-Project',NULL,NULL,'interpretation','deployment_operations','positive','repository_specific',0.3611,10,169,'Section: Document 1 — BookCorpus Story Generation Pipeline

The README describes a pipeline intended to:

install required packages;

import ML/NLP tooling;

inspect GPU availability;

load a BookCorpus subset;

fall back to TinyStories if necessary;

convert the dataset to Pandas;

calculate text-length statistics;

calculate word-count statistics;

visualize distributions;

analyze vocabulary;

calculate readability metrics;

generate a word cloud;

inspect sample texts;

preprocess text;

load GPT-2;

tokenize the dataset;

split train/validation data;

configure a causal-language-model collator;

configure Hugging Face TrainingArguments;

instantiate Trainer;

call trainer.train();

save the intended fine-tuned model;

generate sample stories.

These are documented steps.

No checked-in outputs demonstrate that they were run in this repository.

Section: Document 2 — Alternative Story Datasets Pipeline

second.md keeps much of the same pipeline but changes dataset strategy.

It explicitly recognizes that BookCorpus availability can be problematic.

It provides alternatives including:

TinyStories;

OpenWebText;

WikiText-103;

Daily Dialog.

The code defines a dataset-selection mapping.

It selects a dataset by a single configuration value.

It includes a TinyStories fallback.

