INSERT INTO rag_documents (document_id,repository_index,repository_name,repository_slug,repository_url,retrieval_class,semantic_area,evidence_polarity,evidence_level,specificity_score,concrete_signal_count,word_count,text,topics_json,evidence_areas_json,related_skill_ratings_json,source_fragments_json,provenance_json) VALUES ('repo-128-rd004',128,'Indoor-Journal-',NULL,NULL,'interpretation','performance_scale','positive','repository_specific',0.3676,5,228,'Section: TCP Latency Measurement

The root journal records a TCP SYN measurement command using hping3.

The command is run with:

TCP SYN packets,

port 80,

high-frequency interval,

timestamping through ts,

output logging.

The notes describe measuring in both directions between server and client.

This provides an additional latency-observation method beyond ordinary ICMP ping.

Section: Link Quality and Throughput Over Normalized Motion

linkQuality.py is one of the most important analytical tools in the repository.

Its documented workflow is:

recursively scan a selected experiment root,

identify monitor/timing logs,

identify throughput logs,

identify link-quality JSON logs,

select a monitor file,

choose throughput or link-quality mode,

extract motion events,

filter invalid/short runs,

split alternating runs into forward/backward,

slice network samples by motion windows,

normalize each run to motion progress,

bin normalized progress,

compute per-bin statistics,

export plots and text reports.

Section: Link Quality and Throughput Over Normalized Motion > Motion Normalization

For each run, the tool computes a normalized progress variable:

This is a powerful experimental normalization.

Runs of slightly different wall-clock duration can be compared at the same fraction of trajectory progress.

Section: Link Quality and Throughput Over Normalized Motion > Progress Binning

The tool can aggregate normalized motion into percentage bins.

The documented default interpretation includes 1% progress bins.

Section: Link Quality and Throughput Over Normalized Motion > Bin Statistics

For each bin it computes:

minimum,

maximum,

mean,

